const CBPM='https://www.cbp.gov/travel/international-visitors/money-monetary-instruments';
const CBPK='https://www.cbp.gov/travel/us-citizens/know-before-you-go/know-you-go-traveling-abroad';
const F105='https://fincen105.cbp.dhs.gov/';
const U5316='https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/31/5316';
const U5317='https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/31/5317';
const U5324='https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/31/5324';
const U5332='https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/31/5332';
const FINR='https://www.fincen.gov/resources/statutes-regulations/administrative-rulings/fincen-form-105-currency-and-other-monetary';
const F6059='https://www.cbp.gov/sites/default/files/2024-07/cbp_form_6059b_english_0.pdf';
const UG='https://www.usa.gov/travel-money';
const P=(n,t)=>`[[${n}|${t}]]`;
const pro=t=>`<blockquote><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> ${t}</blockquote>`;
export function usTravel(){return [...usTravel1(),...usTravel2()].map(d=>EXTRA[d.n]?{...d,sections:[...d.sections,...EXTRA[d.n]]}:d);}
function usTravel1(){return [
{n:16,title:'How Much US Cash Can You Travel With Internationally?',crumb:'How much US cash can you travel with',
 h1:'How Much US Cash Can You Travel With Internationally Without Declaring?',kw:'how much US cash can you travel with internationally',
 desc:'How much US cash can you travel with internationally? No legal limit, but report more than $10,000 to CBP on FinCEN Form 105. Rules, forms and penalties.',
 hook:`There is no legal limit on how much US cash you can travel with internationally, but you must report anything over $10,000 to US Customs and Border Protection (CBP). That applies when you enter or leave the United States, and the $10,000 counts all currency and monetary instruments together. The risk of getting it wrong is real: CBP says unreported money can be seized and forfeited, and civil or criminal penalties may follow. This page is part of our ${P(1,'guide to traveling internationally with gold and cash')}.`,
 table:[['Legal limit on carrying cash','None. Carrying any amount is legal (CBP, USA.gov).'],['Reporting threshold','More than $10,000, or the foreign equivalent, at one time.'],['Form','FinCEN Form 105, filed with CBP.'],['Counts toward $10,000','US and foreign coins and currency, traveler\'s checks, money orders, bearer negotiable instruments.'],['Families and groups','The threshold applies to the combined total (CBP).'],['If you do not report','Possible seizure, forfeiture, fines or imprisonment (CBP).']],
 sections:[
 ['The rule in one paragraph',`<p>CBP states that federal law requires you to report amounts exceeding $10,000 when entering or leaving the United States. USA.gov puts the other half plainly: there is no limit to the amount of money you can travel with. The duty is to report, not to stay under a cap.</p><p>The rule covers business trips, sending money abroad and bringing savings home. It also covers people who ship or mail money and people who receive it from abroad.</p>`],
 ['How much US cash can you travel with internationally: what counts toward $10,000',`<p>CBP counts "monetary instruments" together. The list on its page includes US or foreign coins and currency, traveler's checks in any form, and negotiable instruments such as checks, promissory notes and money orders when they are in bearer form, endorsed without restriction, made out to a fictitious payee or otherwise in a form that lets the funds be passed to another person.</p><p>Add them all up. Say you carry $6,000 in bills, a €2,000 bank note bundle and a $3,000 endorsed money order. You are over the line even though no single item is. The foreign cash is measured in its US dollar equivalent, and our guide on ${P(21,'declaring foreign currency at US customs')} covers that case.</p><p>CBP adds that checks or money orders made payable to a named person, not endorsed or carrying restrictive endorsements, are generally not monetary instruments for reporting purposes.</p>`+pro('Write down every item and its value before you leave home. A one-page list makes the FinCEN form much faster, and it shows CBP you were not hiding anything.')],
 ['Entering versus leaving the United States',`<p>The report is required in both directions. A traveler returning to the US files it on arrival, or earlier online. A traveler leaving files it with a CBP officer before departing, using a printed form or the electronic version.</p><p>CBP's Know Before You Go page for people traveling abroad says travelers entering the US with over $10,000 or its foreign equivalent in currency or monetary instruments must file FinCEN Form 105. The page also says electronic filing is available, and that an electronic filer must arrive at a US port of entry within 72 hours or file a new report. Our step-by-step page shows how to ${P(17,'fill out FinCEN Form 105')}.</p>`],
 ['Where gold fits in',`<p>Many readers ask whether gold counts toward the $10,000. Bars and jewelry are goods, not currency, and are handled by the customs declaration (CBP Form 6059B). Coins can be different. FinCEN's ruling on a Canadian silver coin says coin counts as "currency" only if it is legal tender, circulates and is customarily used as a medium of exchange in its country. FinCEN will not rule coin by coin, so the traveler must apply the test. See ${P(7,'traveling with gold coins and customs rules')} and ${P(8,'how much gold you can bring into the USA')}.</p>`],
 ['Three traveler scenarios',`<p><strong>The home buyer.</strong> A traveler flying to Cairo with $14,000 in hundreds to pay a relative must report, because the total is over $10,000. Carrying the cash is lawful. Skipping the report is the problem.</p><p><strong>The family trip.</strong> Two parents and two adults carry $4,000 each, $16,000 in all. CBP says the threshold applies to the combined amount for families and groups, so the group reports.</p><p><strong>The mixed wallet.</strong> A business traveler carries $5,000 cash, a $4,500 money order in bearer form and €1,500 in notes. Everything counts, so the total is above $10,000 and a report is needed.</p>`+pro('If you are unsure whether an item counts, list it on the form and ask the CBP officer. Over-reporting costs you a few minutes. Under-reporting can cost you the money.')],
 ['What happens if you do not report',`<p>CBP says a person who does not file, or who files false or fraudulent information, risks forfeiture and seizure of the money, and may face civil or criminal penalties, including fines or imprisonment. The statutes it cites include 31 U.S.C. 5317 on forfeiture and 31 U.S.C. 5332. Section 5332 covers knowingly concealing more than $10,000 to evade the reporting rule, with up to five years in prison and forfeiture. More detail is in our guide to the ${P(19,'penalty for not declaring cash at customs')}.</p>`],
 ['Splitting cash does not work',`<p>31 U.S.C. 5324 says no person may structure or help structure the importation or exportation of monetary instruments to evade the report. Handing part of your cash to a travel companion so each of you stays under $10,000 can be a violation, and CBP says family or group totals are combined. Read ${P(18,'whether the limit is per person or per family')} before you split anything.</p>`]],
 mistakes:[['Thinking $10,000 is a cap.','It is a reporting trigger. You may carry more if you report it.'],['Counting only paper dollars.','Foreign cash, traveler\'s checks and bearer instruments add to the total.'],['Splitting money among family members.','CBP applies the threshold to the group\'s combined total.'],['Checking cash in a bag and saying nothing.','Concealing money to avoid the report is the conduct section 5332 targets. Declare it to the officer.'],['Waiting past the e-filing window.','An electronic FinCEN report must be followed by arrival at a US port within 72 hours, or you file again.']],
 checklist:['Add up all cash, traveler\'s checks and bearer instruments in US dollar value.','If the total is over $10,000, file FinCEN Form 105 online or on paper.','Keep the confirmation or a copy of the form with your passport.','Carry bank withdrawal slips or sale records that explain where the money came from.','Tell the CBP officer about the money when asked, in your own words.','Check the official CBP page again on the day you travel.'],
 bottom:`<p>You can travel internationally with as much US cash as you own. Over $10,000 in total, you report it on FinCEN Form 105 every time you cross the US border. Count everything, keep the paperwork and go back to the ${P(1,'main guide to traveling with gold and cash')} for the gold side of the trip.</p>`,
 faq:[['Is there a limit on how much cash you can take out of the US?','No. USA.gov says there is no limit on the amount of money you can travel with. Once the total passes $10,000, or the foreign equivalent, you must report it to CBP on FinCEN Form 105 when entering or leaving the country.'],['Does the $10,000 include foreign currency?','Yes. CBP counts US or foreign coins and currency, traveler\'s checks, money orders and bearer negotiable instruments together. Add them in US dollar value. If the combined total is more than $10,000, a report is required, whatever mix of items you carry.'],['Do I have to report money if I am only passing through the US?','The statute covers money transported to or from a place in the United States "from or through" a place outside it. If your trip enters or leaves the US with more than $10,000, assume you must report and ask CBP.'],['Is carrying over $10,000 illegal?','No. CBP says transporting currency of any amount is legal. What is illegal is failing to report an amount over $10,000, filing false or fraudulent information on the report, or knowingly concealing the money to evade the reporting rule. Carrying a large sum openly is not a crime.'],['Where do I file FinCEN Form 105?','CBP recommends filing electronically on the FinCEN Form 105 website. You can also complete a paper form and give it to a CBP officer when leaving, or obtain and complete the form on arrival in the United States. Electronic filing is the faster route.']],
 sources:[[CBPM,'CBP: Money and Other Monetary Instruments'],[CBPK,'CBP: Know Before You Go, traveling abroad'],[UG,'USA.gov: How much money you have to declare'],[F6059,'CBP Form 6059B (customs declaration)'],[FINR,'FinCEN ruling FIN-2011-R001 on the definition of currency'],[U5316,'31 U.S.C. 5316'],[U5317,'31 U.S.C. 5317'],[U5324,'31 U.S.C. 5324'],[U5332,'31 U.S.C. 5332']]}
];}

const CBPP='https://www.cbp.gov/travel/us-citizens/know-before-you-go/prohibited-and-restricted-items';
const C148='https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-19/chapter-I/part-148/subpart-B/section-148.11';
const HTS='https://hts.usitc.gov/reststop/file?filename=Chapter+71&release=2026HTSRev14';
const EXP='https://www.cbp.gov/travel/us-citizens/know-before-you-go/what-expect-when-you-return';
export function usTravel2(){return [
{n:17,title:'How to Fill Out FinCEN Form 105 for Over $10,000 Cash',crumb:'How to fill out FinCEN Form 105',
 h1:'How to Fill Out FinCEN Form 105 When Carrying Over $10,000 in Cash',kw:'how to fill out FinCEN form 105',
 desc:'How to fill out FinCEN Form 105 when carrying over $10,000: who files, what counts, the online steps, the 72-hour rule and the penalties.',
 hook:`To fill out FinCEN Form 105, file it online on CBP's FinCEN 105 website or complete a paper copy and give it to a CBP officer. You need it whenever you carry more than $10,000 in currency or monetary instruments into or out of the United States. A false or missing report can cost you the money, and CBP lists civil and criminal penalties. This walkthrough sits inside our ${P(1,'guide to traveling internationally with gold and cash')} and follows the steps the official site shows.`,
 table:[['Who files','Anyone who brings, sends or receives more than $10,000 in currency or monetary instruments at one time (CBP).'],['Recommended method','Electronic filing on the FinCEN Form 105 website (CBP).'],['Paper option','Print and complete before travel, then give it to a CBP officer (CBP).'],['Time limit for e-filing','Arrive at a US port of entry within 72 hours or file again (CBP).'],['Someone carrying for you','Both of you must report (CBP Form 6059B).'],['False information','Risk of forfeiture and civil or criminal penalties (CBP).']],
 sections:[
 ['What FinCEN Form 105 is',`<p>FinCEN Form 105 is the Report of International Transportation of Currency or Monetary Instruments, often called the CMIR. It was formerly Customs Form 4790, according to CBP's declaration form. CBP's website for the form says it is available to travelers and currency transporters, and to people shipping or receiving money into or out of the United States, when the combined amount is more than $10,000.</p><p>The legal basis is 31 U.S.C. 5316, which requires a report when a person knowingly transports monetary instruments of more than $10,000 at one time into or out of the United States, or receives them from abroad. The amount is explained on our page about ${P(16,'how much US cash you can travel with internationally')}.</p>`],
 ['How to fill out FinCEN Form 105 online: the steps',`<p>The official site shows two paths. A traveler filing for themselves and other travelers follows five steps: start, personal information, origin and destination, monetary instruments, then review and sign. A currency transporter, such as someone shipping money, follows a longer path that adds shipper and receiver details and third-party information.</p><ol><li><strong>Start the report.</strong> Use the FinCEN Form 105 site that CBP recommends. CBP says electronic filing is the quickest approach.</li><li><strong>Personal information.</strong> Enter your details as they appear on your travel document.</li><li><strong>Origin and destination.</strong> Say where the money is coming from and where it is going.</li><li><strong>Monetary instruments.</strong> List the type and amount of currency and each other instrument. The paper form asks you to specify the type, issuing entity and date, and a serial or other identifying number for other monetary instruments.</li><li><strong>Review and sign.</strong> Check every number against your list, then submit.</li></ol>`+pro('Prepare a one-page list of every note bundle, traveler\'s check and money order with its amount and serial number before you open the form. Typing from a list avoids mistakes that become false statements.')],
 ['The 72-hour rule for electronic reports',`<p>CBP's travel guidance says a traveler who files electronically must arrive at a US port of entry within 72 hours. A traveler who misses the 72-hour deadline must submit a new electronic FinCEN Form 105. If your flight is delayed or you change plans, check the time before you rely on a report you filed days earlier.</p><p>CBP also says travelers using Mobile Passport Control for entry get the chance to submit their declaration and file the report electronically in the same process.</p>`],
 ['Paper filing and when to do it',`<p>CBP's money page describes paper options as well: print and fill out the form before you travel and present it to a CBP officer before departing the United States, or obtain and complete the form before leaving or on arrival. Leaving the country with the money works the same way as entering it: the report is still required, and you give it to CBP before you depart.</p>`],
 ['If someone else carries the money, or you receive it',`<p>CBP's Form 6059B says that if you have someone else carry the currency or monetary instrument for you, you must also file a report. The statute adds a duty for a person who receives more than $10,000 in monetary instruments transported into the US from abroad. If you are the one on the receiving end and no report was filed, that duty is yours.</p>`],
 ['What counts as a monetary instrument on the form',`<p>CBP lists US or foreign coins and currency, traveler's checks, negotiable instruments in bearer or similar form, incomplete signed instruments with the payee omitted, and securities or stock in bearer form. Our guide to ${P(21,'declaring foreign currency at US customs')} shows how to value foreign cash, and the ${P(7,'gold coin rules')} explain when a coin may count as currency.</p>`+pro('If the officer asks how much you carry before you file, answer with the exact total from your list. A match between your answer and your form is the best proof you were open about it.')]],
 mistakes:[['Estimating the amount.','Use exact figures from a list. A report that differs from what you carry can be treated as false.'],['Filing electronically and traveling a week later.','The 72-hour window applies to the electronic report.'],['Leaving the other carrier off.','If someone carries money for you, CBP says you must report too.'],['Treating checks as exempt.','Checks made out to a named person and not endorsed are generally not instruments, but bearer or endorsed ones are.'],['Reporting only on arrival when leaving.','On departure you file with CBP before you leave the US.']],
 checklist:['Count all cash and instruments and write each amount and serial number.','Choose electronic filing on the FinCEN 105 site or a paper form.','Save the confirmation or a copy of the form.','Check the 72-hour window against your flight time.','Bring ID and proof of the source of funds.','Show the confirmation to CBP and answer questions plainly.'],
 bottom:`<p>Knowing how to fill out FinCEN Form 105 takes about ten minutes if your list is ready. File electronically, keep the confirmation, and watch the 72-hour rule. For the wider picture, including gold, go to the ${P(1,'main travel guide')}.</p>`,
 faq:[['Is FinCEN Form 105 the same as the customs declaration?','No. FinCEN Form 105 reports currency and monetary instruments over $10,000. The customs declaration, CBP Form 6059B, lists goods you bring back. A traveler carrying both goods and more than $10,000 in monetary instruments may need to complete both forms.'],['Can I file FinCEN Form 105 online?','Yes. CBP says electronic filing on the FinCEN Form 105 website is the recommended and quickest approach. An electronic filer must arrive at a US port of entry within 72 hours, or the report must be filed again. Keep the confirmation with your passport.'],['Do I file it when leaving the United States?','Yes. The $10,000 rule applies when entering or leaving. CBP says you can print and complete the form before travel and present it to a CBP officer before departing, or obtain the form and complete it before you leave. Keep a copy.'],['What if I carry money for a friend?','Form 6059B says that if you have someone else carry the money for you, you must also file a report. Each person with a duty should report. Ask CBP before you travel if your arrangement is unclear, and keep a copy of every report.'],['What if I make a mistake on the form?','CBP says submitting false or fraudulent information risks forfeiture and seizure, and possible civil or criminal penalties. If you spot an error before travel, correct it. If you spot it at the border, tell the CBP officer right away and keep your copy.']],
 sources:[[CBPM,'CBP: Money and Other Monetary Instruments'],[F105,'CBP: FinCEN Form 105 e-filing site'],[CBPK,'CBP: Know Before You Go, traveling abroad'],[F6059,'CBP Form 6059B'],[U5316,'31 U.S.C. 5316']]},
{n:18,title:'$10,000 Customs Limit: Per Person or Per Family?',crumb:'$10,000 limit per person or per family',
 h1:'Does the $10,000 Customs Limit Apply Per Person or Per Family?',kw:'10000 customs limit per person or family',
 desc:'The $10,000 customs limit per person or family: CBP applies it to the combined total for families and groups. Splitting cash can be an offense.',
 hook:`The $10,000 customs limit is not per person when you travel as a family or group. CBP says the threshold applies to the total amount the group carries together, so a family that holds $16,000 across four wallets must report. Splitting money to stay under the line can itself break the law, and the money can be forfeited. This answer is one part of our ${P(1,'guide to traveling internationally with gold and cash')}.`,
 table:[['Per person or per family?','Combined total for families and groups traveling together (CBP).'],['Threshold','More than $10,000 or the foreign equivalent.'],['Splitting cash to avoid the form','Structuring is prohibited by 31 U.S.C. 5324.'],['Who files','Check with CBP; a report may be needed for the group.'],['Form','FinCEN Form 105.'],['Penalties','Seizure, forfeiture, fines or imprisonment (CBP).']],
 sections:[
 ['What CBP says about families and groups',`<p>CBP's money page says that when families or groups are involved, the $10,000 threshold applies to the total amount they are carrying or sending collectively, not per individual. CBP's guidance for travelers returning to the US says the same: the threshold applies to the total carried by an individual or by groups traveling together, for example families, where the combined amount exceeds $10,000.</p><p>So the question "does the $10,000 customs limit apply per person or family" has a direct answer. For people traveling together, the family total counts.</p>`],
 ['The 10000 customs limit per person or family in practice',`<p>Take a couple with two adult children. The mother carries $3,000, the father $4,000 and each child $4,500. No single person is above $10,000, but the group carries $16,000. CBP says the group total counts, so a report is required.</p><p>The pages we checked do not set a separate allowance for each child or each adult, and they do not say who in the group must sign. If your family is in that position, say so to the CBP officer and ask how they want the report filed. Our walkthrough shows how to ${P(17,'fill out FinCEN Form 105')}.</p>`+pro('Decide before the trip who carries the money, and have that person file one clear report that names the group. Do not shuffle cash between pockets in the airport.')],
 ['Why splitting cash can be a crime',`<p>31 U.S.C. 5324 says no person may, to evade the report, structure or assist in structuring, or attempt to structure, any importation or exportation of monetary instruments. If a family hands out $9,500 each to stay under the form, the group is no better off than before, and the splitting itself can be treated as the offense.</p><p>The criminal law on bulk cash smuggling, 31 U.S.C. 5332, covers knowingly concealing more than $10,000 to evade the report. Our page on the ${P(19,'penalty for not declaring cash at customs')} covers what that can mean.</p>`],
 ['What if you travel separately',`<p>CBP's wording covers groups traveling together. The statute defines the duty by the amount transported "at one time", and it lets the Treasury count closely related events together. If two relatives fly on different days with money from the same source for the same purpose, treat that as a question for CBP before you go rather than a gap to use.</p>`],
 ['Foreign cash and other instruments in the group total',`<p>The $10,000 is measured across US and foreign currency, traveler's checks, money orders and bearer instruments. Convert foreign money to US dollars for the total, and read ${P(21,'what to do about foreign currency at US customs')}. Our main page on ${P(16,'how much US cash you can travel with internationally')} lists the full set.</p>`]],
 mistakes:[['Reading $10,000 as a personal allowance.','For families and groups, CBP counts the combined total.'],['Splitting cash to dodge the form.','Structuring to evade the report is prohibited.'],['Leaving the youngest traveler out of the count.','CBP does not carve out children on the pages checked; count what the group carries.'],['Not telling the officer the money is shared.','If the money belongs to the whole family, say so and let CBP guide the form.'],['Assuming a gift removes the duty.','Money you carry for someone else still counts when you cross the border.']],
 checklist:['Add up every traveler\'s cash and instruments.','If the group total is above $10,000, plan one FinCEN Form 105 report.','Agree in advance who carries the money.','Bring proof of the source of funds and the purpose.','Do not move cash between travelers to reduce a number.','Check CBP\'s page again before the trip.'],
 bottom:`<p>On the 10000 customs limit per person or family, the answer is simple: the family or group total counts. Report openly, keep one clear list, and do not split. The ${P(1,'main guide')} ties this to gold, coins and exchange tips.</p>`,
 faq:[['Is the $10,000 customs limit per person?','CBP says that when families or groups are involved, the $10,000 threshold applies to the total amount they carry or send collectively, not per individual. Travelers in a group that carries more than $10,000 in total should plan to report.'],['Can each family member carry $9,000 without reporting?','No. CBP applies the threshold to the group total, so four people carrying $9,000 each hold $36,000 together. Splitting to avoid the report can also fall under 31 U.S.C. 5324, which prohibits structuring imports or exports of monetary instruments. Report openly instead.'],['Do children count?','The CBP pages we checked do not exempt children or set a separate allowance. They refer to groups traveling together, such as families. Count the cash and instruments carried by everyone in the group, and ask the officer if you are unsure.'],['Do I have to report if we travel on different flights?','CBP states the rule for groups traveling together. The statute looks at money transported at one time and lets Treasury combine closely related events. If you split a family trip across flights, ask CBP first and do not rely on the gap.'],['Where do I report family cash?','File FinCEN Form 105 with CBP, online or on paper, and present it to a CBP officer when entering or leaving. CBP says electronic filing is the quickest way. Mention that the money belongs to the group, and keep a copy of the filing.']],
 sources:[[CBPM,'CBP: Money and Other Monetary Instruments'],[CBPK,'CBP: Know Before You Go, traveling abroad'],[U5316,'31 U.S.C. 5316'],[U5324,'31 U.S.C. 5324'],[U5332,'31 U.S.C. 5332']]},
{n:19,title:'Penalty for Not Declaring Cash at Customs: What to Know',crumb:'Penalty for not declaring cash',
 h1:'What Happens If You Don\'t Declare Cash Over $10,000 at Customs?',kw:'penalty for not declaring cash at customs',
 desc:'Penalty for not declaring cash at customs: CBP says money can be seized and forfeited, with civil or criminal penalties. Concealment can mean 5 years.',
 hook:`If you do not declare cash over $10,000 at customs, CBP can seize it and the money can be forfeited. CBP's own page also lists civil or criminal penalties, including fines or imprisonment, for a missing or false report. Carrying the cash is legal. The penalty for not declaring cash at customs applies to the missing report, a false report or hidden money. This page belongs to our ${P(1,'guide to traveling internationally with gold and cash')}.`,
 table:[['What can happen','Seizure and forfeiture of the money (CBP).'],['Other consequences','Civil or criminal penalties, including fines or imprisonment (CBP).'],['Concealment to evade the report','Up to 5 years in prison plus forfeiture (31 U.S.C. 5332).'],['Civil forfeiture','Property involved in a violation can be seized and forfeited (31 U.S.C. 5317, 5332).'],['Splitting cash','Structuring is prohibited (31 U.S.C. 5324).'],['How to avoid it','Report on FinCEN Form 105 and be accurate.']],
 sections:[
 ['What CBP says the consequences are',`<p>CBP says that an individual who fails to file a report, or who submits false or fraudulent information, risks having their currency or monetary instruments forfeited and seized. In some instances, CBP adds, they may face civil or criminal penalties such as fines or imprisonment. The page cites 31 U.S.C. 5321 and 31 CFR 1010.820, 31 U.S.C. 5322 and 31 CFR 1010.840, 31 U.S.C. 5317 and 31 CFR 1010.830, and 31 U.S.C. 5332.</p><p>That is the full official list on the page we checked. CBP's page does not state a fixed dollar amount for civil penalties, so we do not give one.</p>`],
 ['The penalty for not declaring cash at customs: seizure and forfeiture',`<p>Under 31 U.S.C. 5317(c), any property involved in a violation of the reporting rule in section 5316, and any property traceable to it, may be seized and forfeited to the United States under civil forfeiture procedures. A court that sentences someone for a violation of section 5316 must order forfeiture of the property involved.</p><p>In plain terms, the money you failed to report is the first thing at risk. That is why a traveler who forgets the form can lose more than a fine's worth of cash.</p>`+pro('If you realize at the airport that you forgot to report, tell the CBP officer before inspection starts. Honest disclosure is the best position to be in.')],
 ['Bulk cash smuggling: when concealment makes it criminal',`<p>31 U.S.C. 5332 makes it an offense to knowingly conceal more than $10,000 in currency or monetary instruments, with intent to evade the reporting requirement, and transport it into or out of the United States. The text says concealment includes hiding cash in clothing, luggage, a backpack or another container carried by the person. A conviction carries imprisonment of not more than five years, and the court must order forfeiture of the property involved. A civil forfeiture route also exists.</p>`],
 ['Structuring the trip to stay under the line',`<p>31 U.S.C. 5324 prohibits structuring, or helping to structure, an importation or exportation of monetary instruments to evade the report. If you split cash among relatives, CBP still counts the group total. Our page on the ${P(18,'$10,000 limit per person or family')} explains the group rule.</p>`],
 ['How to avoid the problem',`<p>File the report accurately and in time. The steps are in our guide on how to ${P(17,'fill out FinCEN Form 105')}. Remember the amount is measured across all cash and instruments, as ${P(16,'our overview of carrying US cash internationally')} explains. And answer CBP's questions truthfully, because false information is a separate trigger for penalties.</p>`+pro('Keep documents showing where the money came from, such as a bank withdrawal slip or a sale contract. They do not replace the report, but they help explain a large amount.')]],
 mistakes:[['Hiding cash "just in case".','Section 5332 targets knowing concealment to evade the report, with up to five years in prison.'],['Giving a lower number.','CBP lists false or fraudulent information as a reason for forfeiture and penalties.'],['Splitting between travelers.','Group totals count and structuring is prohibited.'],['Assuming a small mistake will be ignored.','CBP says seizure and forfeiture are risks even for a missing report.'],['Not keeping paperwork.','If funds are held, records of the source can matter when you respond.']],
 checklist:['Count all cash and instruments before leaving.','File FinCEN Form 105 if the total is over $10,000.','Keep a copy of the report and ID.','Carry documents showing the source of funds.','Tell the officer the truth about the total.','If the money is seized, ask for the paperwork CBP issues and talk to a lawyer.'],
 bottom:`<p>The penalty for not declaring cash at customs can include losing the money and facing civil or criminal consequences, according to CBP. The fix is simple: report over $10,000, be accurate, and never conceal or split the money. See the ${P(1,'complete travel guide')} for the full gold and cash picture.</p>`,
 faq:[['Will CBP take my money if I forget to declare it?','CBP says a person who fails to file a report risks having the currency or monetary instruments forfeited and seized. Whether the money is seized in a particular case is up to CBP. If you realize you forgot, tell the officer before inspection.'],['Is not declaring cash a crime?','CBP says failure to file or false information can bring civil or criminal penalties, including fines or imprisonment. 31 U.S.C. 5332 makes knowing concealment of more than $10,000 to evade the report a crime, with up to five years in prison.'],['Can I get seized cash back?','The sources we checked describe seizure and forfeiture procedures but do not promise a result. The path depends on the case. If your money is seized, keep every document CBP gives you and consult a lawyer who handles customs forfeiture.'],['Does it matter if the money is legal?','Carrying legal money is lawful, but CBP says unreported money over $10,000 can be forfeited. The report is a separate duty from the legality of the funds. Report even when the source is clearly legitimate, and keep records that explain it.'],['What about splitting the cash among family?','31 U.S.C. 5324 prohibits structuring an importation or exportation of monetary instruments to evade the report. CBP also applies the $10,000 threshold to the combined total of families and groups. Splitting does not remove the duty to report. Report the total openly.']],
 sources:[[CBPM,'CBP: Money and Other Monetary Instruments'],[U5317,'31 U.S.C. 5317'],[U5324,'31 U.S.C. 5324'],[U5332,'31 U.S.C. 5332'],[U5316,'31 U.S.C. 5316']]},
{n:21,title:'Declare Foreign Currency at US Customs: The $10,000 Rule',crumb:'Declare foreign currency at US customs',
 h1:'Do You Have to Declare Foreign Currency Equivalent to $10,000 USD?',kw:'declare foreign currency at US customs',
 desc:'Declare foreign currency at US customs when the total is more than $10,000 or its foreign equivalent. Learn what counts, how to total it and how to file.',
 hook:`Yes. You must declare foreign currency at US customs when you carry more than $10,000, or the foreign equivalent, in total. CBP says the rule covers US or foreign currency and other monetary instruments, added together. The risk of ignoring it is seizure and forfeiture of the money, plus possible penalties. This page is part of our ${P(1,'guide to traveling internationally with gold and cash')} and shows how to total mixed currencies.`,
 table:[['Does foreign cash count?','Yes. US or foreign currency counts (CBP).'],['Threshold','More than $10,000 or its foreign equivalent.'],['Mixed currencies','Add them together in US dollar value.'],['Form','FinCEN Form 105.'],['Official exchange rate on the CBP page?','Not stated on the pages we checked.'],['If near the line','Report to be safe.']],
 sections:[
 ['What the rules say about foreign currency',`<p>CBP's travel page says all travelers entering the United States with over $10,000, or its foreign equivalent, in currency or monetary instruments must file FinCEN Form 105. Its list of reportable items includes US or foreign currency, traveler's checks, money orders and bearer negotiable instruments. CBP Form 6059B words it as more than $10,000 in US or foreign equivalent, or a combination of both.</p><p>If you want to declare foreign currency at US customs, think in totals, not currencies. Euros, pounds, pesos and dollars all go in one sum.</p>`],
 ['How to declare foreign currency at US customs: totaling mixed money',`<p>The sources we checked do not set an official conversion rate. They say "foreign equivalent" without naming a table. A practical approach is to convert each currency at a published rate from your bank or a major rate source on the day you travel, write down the rate and the source, and add the results. If your total is close to $10,000, report. A cautious report costs a few minutes.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>Amount</th><th>Counts toward the total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>US dollars</td><td>$4,000</td><td>$4,000</td></tr><tr><td>Euro notes</td><td>€3,000</td><td>Converted to USD at your recorded rate</td></tr><tr><td>Traveler's checks</td><td>$2,500</td><td>$2,500</td></tr><tr><td>Bearer money order</td><td>$1,000</td><td>$1,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In this example the euro notes alone decide whether the group goes over. The total is $7,500 plus the converted euros, so more than $2,500 worth of euros triggers the report.</p>`+pro('Screenshot the exchange rate you used, with the date and the source. If an officer asks how you valued your euros, you can answer in seconds.')],
 ['What counts as foreign currency',`<p>FinCEN's rule defines "currency" as the coin and paper money of the United States or of any other country that is designated as legal tender, circulates and is customarily used and accepted as a medium of exchange in the country of issuance. It lists US silver certificates, US notes, Federal Reserve notes and official foreign bank notes customarily used in a foreign country. Coins and notes must meet all three conditions. FinCEN says it will not confirm case by case whether a coin qualifies.</p><p>Old withdrawn bank notes and collector items raise that test. Gold coins are a special case, covered in ${P(7,'traveling with gold coins')}.</p>`],
 ['Entering versus leaving',`<p>The report applies to money brought into or taken out of the United States. A traveler leaving with foreign currency bought for the trip also has to count it. Anyone who receives such money from abroad can have a duty too. See ${P(17,'how to fill out FinCEN Form 105')} for the filing steps.</p>`],
 ['Foreign currency and customs for goods',`<p>Foreign money is not "merchandise," so it is not listed on the customs goods declaration. But CBP Form 6059B asks about both. If you carry gold jewelry, coins or bars, the goods rules in ${P(8,'how much gold you can bring into the USA')} apply on top of the cash report.</p>`]],
 mistakes:[['Counting only dollars.','Foreign notes, traveler\'s checks and money orders all count.'],['Using a guess for the exchange rate.','Record a rate and its source for your total.'],['Skipping the form because each currency is small.','The $10,000 is the combined sum.'],['Assuming coins never count.','Legal tender coins that circulate can be currency; ask when unsure.'],['Forgetting the way out.','Leaving with foreign currency counts too.']],
 checklist:['List each currency and amount.','Convert at a published rate and record the source.','Add traveler\'s checks and bearer instruments.','File FinCEN Form 105 if the total passes $10,000.','Carry proof of where the money came from.','Check CBP\'s page again before you fly.'],
 bottom:`<p>To declare foreign currency at US customs, add everything together in US dollar value and report anything over $10,000. When you are close, report. Use the ${P(1,'main guide')} for how gold and cash rules fit together on one trip.</p>`,
 faq:[['Does foreign currency count toward the $10,000 reporting limit?','Yes. CBP lists US or foreign currency among the reportable items, along with traveler\'s checks, money orders and bearer negotiable instruments. If the total of all of them is more than $10,000, or its foreign equivalent, you must file FinCEN Form 105.'],['What exchange rate should I use?','The CBP pages we checked say "foreign equivalent" without naming a rate. A practical choice is a published bank or market rate on the travel date. Write down the rate and its source, and report if your total is close to $10,000.'],['Do I declare foreign currency if I am leaving the US?','Yes. The reporting duty applies when entering or leaving the United States. A traveler leaving with more than $10,000 in US or foreign currency, or a mix, must file FinCEN Form 105 with CBP before departing. Keep a copy of the form with your travel papers.'],['Is a foreign gold coin foreign currency?','Only if it meets FinCEN\'s three tests: legal tender, circulating, and customarily used as a medium of exchange in its country. FinCEN will not rule coin by coin. Bullion coins sold at a premium to collectors usually raise doubt, so ask CBP.'],['What if I forget to include foreign cash?','CBP says false or missing reports risk forfeiture and seizure of the money and possible civil or criminal penalties. If you realize the mistake at the airport, tell the CBP officer before inspection, correct the report, and keep your records.']],
 sources:[[CBPK,'CBP: Know Before You Go, traveling abroad'],[CBPM,'CBP: Money and Other Monetary Instruments'],[F6059,'CBP Form 6059B'],[FINR,'FinCEN ruling FIN-2011-R001'],[F105,'CBP: FinCEN Form 105 e-filing site']]},
{n:8,title:'How Much Gold Can You Bring Into the USA?',crumb:'How much gold can you bring into the USA',
 h1:'How Much Gold Can You Bring Into the USA Without Paying Duty?',kw:'how much gold can you bring into the USA',
 desc:'How much gold can you bring into the USA? CBP lists no quantity cap, but you must declare it. Bullion is duty-free in the tariff. Origin limits apply.',
 hook:`The CBP page we checked sets no quantity cap on gold you bring into the USA, but you must declare it. CBP says gold coins, medals and bullion may be brought in, except items tied to certain sanctioned countries. Gold bullion is listed as duty-free in the tariff schedule, while jewelry and other items can be dutiable. Skipping the declaration risks forfeiture. This page is part of our ${P(1,'guide to traveling internationally with gold and cash')}.`,
 table:[['Quantity limit on gold','No cap stated on the CBP page checked.'],['Must you declare it?','Yes. All articles must be declared (19 CFR 148.11; CBP Form 6059B).'],['Bullion duty','Free in the 2026 tariff, heading 7108.12.10 (HTSUS).'],['Prohibited origins','Items originating in or brought from Cuba, Iran, Burma and most of Sudan (CBP, OFAC).'],['Gold copies','Prohibited unless marked with the country of issuance (CBP).'],['Cash-like coins','May also need FinCEN Form 105 above $10,000.']],
 sections:[
 ['What CBP says about gold',`<p>CBP's page on prohibited and restricted items says that gold coins, medals and bullion, formerly prohibited, may be brought into the United States. It adds that under regulations administered by the Office of Foreign Assets Control, such items originating in or brought from Cuba, Iran, Burma (Myanmar) and most of Sudan are prohibited entry. Copies of gold coins are prohibited if they are not properly marked by country of issuance.</p><p>The page does not set a weight or value cap. That does not make gold exempt from reporting. It means the control is on origin and declaration, not quantity.</p>`],
 ['How much gold can you bring into the USA: the declaration duty',`<p>19 CFR 148.11 says all articles brought into the United States by any individual must be declared to a CBP officer at the port of first arrival. CBP Form 6059B is the written declaration. CBP also warns that if you do not declare something that should have been declared, you risk forfeiting the item. For gold, declare the bars, coins and jewelry, and keep receipts.</p>`+pro('Bring the original invoice or assay certificate for each bar. It shows weight, purity and value in one page and makes the declaration quick.')],
 ['Duty: bullion versus jewelry',`<p>The 2026 US tariff schedule, chapter 71, shows nonmonetary gold bullion and doré under 7108.12.10 with a general rate of Free, and monetary gold under 7108.20 as Free. Jewelry is classified elsewhere in chapter 71, where rates vary by item, so a necklace is not treated like a bar. For personal-use goods, CBP's returning traveler rules give a duty-free exemption of $200, $800 or $1,600, depending on the countries visited, if you meet its conditions: at least 48 hours abroad, items with you, declared, and no use of the exemption in the past 30 days.</p><p>Check the current tariff line for your item on the USITC site, or ask a CBP officer. Our page on ${P(5,'declaring personal gold jewelry')} covers the jewelry side.</p>`],
 ['When the $10,000 cash rule can apply',`<p>FinCEN's rules define currency as legal tender coin and paper money that circulates and is customarily used as a medium of exchange in its country. A coin that passes all three tests is currency, and over $10,000 it needs FinCEN Form 105. Bars and jewelry are goods and follow the customs declaration. Read ${P(7,'traveling with gold coins and customs rules')} for the split.</p>`],
 ['Gold from Dubai or other hubs',`<p>The same declaration rules apply wherever you buy the gold. Our guides to ${P(11,'bringing gold from Dubai to the USA')} and ${P(10,'traveling to Dubai with gold')} cover the other end of the trip.</p>`]],
 mistakes:[['Assuming no cap means no declaration.','CBP requires declaring all articles. The cap question is separate.'],['Skipping receipts.','Value in US dollars goes on the form, so receipts matter.'],['Ignoring origin.','Items from Cuba, Iran, Burma and most of Sudan are prohibited under OFAC rules, per CBP.'],['Treating jewelry like bullion.','Jewelry follows other tariff lines and the duty-free exemption rules.'],['Forgetting the cash form for coins.','A legal tender coin over $10,000 may be reportable currency.']],
 checklist:['List each bar, coin and jewelry piece with weight and value.','Keep invoices, assay certificates and receipts together.','Check origin against OFAC restrictions.','Complete CBP Form 6059B truthfully.','Add FinCEN Form 105 if monetary instruments exceed $10,000.','Review the current tariff line before you fly.'],
 bottom:`<p>On how much gold you can bring into the USA, the official answer is no stated quantity cap, a duty-free tariff line for bullion, and a firm duty to declare. Back it with receipts, check origin, and use the ${P(1,'main guide')} for the full cash and gold checklist.</p>`,
 faq:[['Is there a limit on bringing gold into the US?','The CBP page we checked sets no weight or value cap on gold coins, medals and bullion. It does restrict items originating in or brought from Cuba, Iran, Burma and most of Sudan. All articles still need to be declared to a CBP officer.'],['Do I pay duty on gold bars?','The 2026 tariff schedule lists nonmonetary gold bullion and doré under 7108.12.10 with a general rate of Free. Other gold items, including jewelry, follow different tariff lines. Check the current schedule or ask CBP for your item. The duty-free traveler exemption may also apply to personal items.'],['Do I have to declare gold if it is under $10,000?','Yes. 19 CFR 148.11 says all articles brought into the United States must be declared. The $10,000 line triggers FinCEN Form 105 for currency and monetary instruments. It does not remove the customs declaration for goods. Receipts help you give an accurate value.'],['Do gold coins count as cash?','Only legal tender coins that circulate and are customarily used as money in their country count as currency under FinCEN\'s test. Collector bullion coins often do not qualify. FinCEN will not rule coin by coin, so ask CBP if unsure.'],['What happens if I do not declare gold?','CBP says if you do not declare something that should have been declared, you risk forfeiting the item. Declare bars, coins and jewelry, and keep receipts. For monetary instruments over $10,000, missing reports can bring seizure and penalties. Early, honest declaration is the safest route.']],
 sources:[[CBPP,'CBP: Prohibited and restricted items (gold)'],[C148,'19 CFR 148.11 Declaration required'],[EXP,'CBP: What to expect when you return'],[CBPK,'CBP: Know Before You Go, traveling abroad'],[HTS,'USITC HTSUS 2026 Revision 14, chapter 71'],[FINR,'FinCEN ruling FIN-2011-R001'],[F6059,'CBP Form 6059B']]},
];}

const EXTRA={
8:[['Step by step: declaring gold on arrival',`<p>CBP's guidance for returning travelers says every traveler completes CBP Declaration Form 6059B. It lists three ways to do it: a paper form, the online form at an Automated Passport Control kiosk, or the Global Entry mobile app for Trusted Traveler members. CBP suggests keeping purchase receipts in an envelope in your carry-on, and says that if warranted the officer will calculate the duties on newly acquired goods.</p><ol><li>Before landing, list each gold item with weight, purity and value in US dollars.</li><li>Fill out Form 6059B or the kiosk form and tick that you carry items acquired abroad.</li><li>Keep the receipts and certificates in your carry-on, not in checked luggage.</li><li>Show the declaration and answer the officer's questions plainly.</li><li>If monetary instruments are above $10,000, hand over FinCEN Form 105 as well.</li><li>Pay any duty the officer assesses and keep the receipt.</li></ol>`+pro('Keep the gold itself in your carry-on and the paperwork on top. If an officer asks to see an item, you can show it without opening your checked bag at the carousel.')],
 ['Sanctions and marking rules to check before you buy',`<p>CBP's page ties the entry rule to Office of Foreign Assets Control regulations. Gold coins, medals and bullion originating in or brought from Cuba, Iran, Burma (Myanmar) and most of Sudan are prohibited. If you bought gold in a third country, ask the seller for documents that show where it was made or refined. CBP also prohibits copies of gold coins that are not properly marked by country of issuance, so replicas without a mark are a problem even when the metal is real gold.</p><p>Sanctions lists change. Look at the current OFAC and CBP pages the week you travel instead of relying on an older article, including this one.</p>`],
 ['A worked example',`<p>A traveler returns from Dubai with a 100 g bar and a gold chain. The bar's invoice shows 99.99% purity and a value in dirhams, and the chain's receipt shows weight and price. At the kiosk she declares both, converts the values to dollars on the form, and keeps the receipts handy. The bar falls under bullion, and the chain is jewelry whose duty depends on its tariff line and on any traveler exemption she can use. Neither item is capped by weight on CBP's page. Both must be declared. For the Dubai side, see ${P(11,'bringing gold from Dubai to the USA')}.</p>`+pro('Write the dollar value next to each item on your own list the night before. Converting at the kiosk with a line in the queue is where mistakes happen.')]],
18:[['Group scenarios and what to ask CBP',`<p>Families rarely fit a tidy rule, so use these as starting points and confirm with the officer.</p><ul><li><strong>Parents and young children.</strong> The pages we checked refer to groups traveling together, such as families, and do not carve out children. Count what the group carries and state who holds it.</li><li><strong>Two adults with joint savings.</strong> If both hold part of the same savings, the combined total is the number.</li><li><strong>Relatives meeting abroad.</strong> If you each fly in from different places and combine the money in the US, the money is crossing the border on separate trips. Treat each person's own crossing as its own question, and ask CBP when the funds clearly belong together.</li><li><strong>A friend carrying money for you.</strong> CBP's Form 6059B says you must also file a report in that case.</li></ul>`+pro('When money truly belongs to the whole family, name it that way on the form and say it at the desk. Saying it plainly looks like what it is: an open report.')],
 ['How a group report works in practice',`<p>The sources we checked describe the rule but not a family-specific form. The practical route is the one CBP gives everyone: file FinCEN Form 105 electronically or on paper, list the instruments and amounts, and present it to a CBP officer. Our step-by-step page on how to ${P(17,'fill out FinCEN Form 105')} shows the fields. If the officer wants separate reports for individuals, follow that instruction and keep copies of everything you are given.</p><p>Remember that 31 U.S.C. 5316 puts the duty on the person who transports the money and on the person who receives it from abroad. Either role can create a reporting duty.</p>`]],
21:[['Worked examples with mixed currencies',`<p>These examples use made-up exchange rates to show the arithmetic. They are not current rates.</p><p><strong>Example one.</strong> A family returns with $3,000, €5,000 and £2,000. At an example rate of $1.10 per euro and $1.30 per pound, the euros are $5,500 and the pounds $2,600. The total is $11,100, so the family reports.</p><p><strong>Example two.</strong> A traveler carries 6,000 Canadian dollars and $3,000 US. At an example rate of $0.70 per Canadian dollar, the total is $7,200. That is under $10,000, so no FinCEN report is needed. The traveler still declares goods on the customs form as usual.</p><p><strong>Example three.</strong> A traveler holds $9,800 in cash and a $400 bearer money order. Each item is under the line. Together they are $10,200, so a report is needed.</p>`+pro('Round up when you are close. If the true figure is $9,950 and your rate source is a few cents off, reporting costs nothing, while a wrong guess can cost the cash.')],
 ['Coins, bank notes and collector items',`<p>FinCEN's definition asks whether the coin or note is legal tender, circulates and is customarily used as a medium of exchange in the country that issued it. A modern foreign bank note in current use meets that test. A collector coin sold at a premium that no one uses for shopping may not. FinCEN's own ruling says it will not confirm individual coin types, so you must apply the test using public information, such as the issuing country's coinage law and how the item is distributed. When in doubt, report. Our page on ${P(7,'traveling with gold coins')} covers the same test for gold.</p>`]],
19:[['A worked example',`<p>A traveler lands with $12,500 in cash, tucked into a jacket lining, and ticks "no" on the money question at the kiosk. An officer asks if he carries more than $10,000. If he says no and the money is found, he faces the three problems CBP names: a missing report, false information, and, because the money was hidden, the concealment that 31 U.S.C. 5332 describes. Compare that with a traveler who carries $12,500 in an open wallet, files the form and answers questions: the money is lawful and the paperwork is complete.</p>`+pro('If you ever see the words "bulk cash smuggling" in a document, stop and call a lawyer who handles customs and forfeiture cases. Do not sign anything you do not understand.')],
 ['What to do if CBP seizes money',`<p>The sources we checked describe seizure and forfeiture but do not list a recovery timetable. If CBP holds money, ask for the written notice and receipt. Keep every document, including your FinCEN form, bank slips and any contract that explains the money. The forfeiture procedures cited by the statutes include civil routes, so a lawyer experienced in federal forfeiture can tell you the options that apply to your case. We cannot give you legal advice, and no article can.</p>`]],
};
